        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Kursk</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">5 July to 23 August 1943: the German offensive, Operation Citadel, began on 5 July and was ended on 17 July in the south; the Soviet counter-offensives began on 12 July and 3 August, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> was liberated on 23 August</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> salient, near Kursk in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with fighting on its northern face at Ponyri and Olkhovatka and on its southern face at Prokhorovka</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under Hitler, with Field Marshal Erich von Manstein (Army Group South) and Field Marshal Günther von Kluge (Army Group Centre), about 777,000 to 779,000 men at the start; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under Stalin, with Zhukov, Vatutin, Rokossovsky and Konev, about 1,910,361 men including the Steppe Front reserve</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The German offensive was halted before it broke through, reaching a depth of 8 to 12 kilometres in the north and 35 kilometres in the south. Soviet counter-offensives at Orel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> followed, and the Soviets kept the initiative for the rest of the war.</span></div>
        </div>
