    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Soviet intelligence, from the Lucy spy ring and from decrypts forwarded by John Cairncross, gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> months of warning, and it built a defence in depth of six belts, with 503,993 anti-tank mines laid.</li><li>German losses are hard to fix: Frieser counts 54,182 casualties during Citadel itself, while other historians put losses over the whole battle at 380,000 to 430,000 or more than half a million.</li><li>Hitler ended the offensive after a week, in part because the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> never launched another major offensive on the Eastern Front.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 8 July German ground-attack aircraft armed with 30 mm MK 103 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> destroyed at least 50 tanks of the Soviet 2nd Guards Tank Corps, the first time in military history that an attacking tank formation was defeated by air power alone.</p></aside>
    </section>

