    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">February 1943</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> is retaken by the Soviets on 8 February, and Manstein's counter-stroke later retakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> and Belgorod, leaving a Soviet salient centred on Kursk</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1943</span><span class="rf-text">Hitler orders Operation Citadel on 15 April, and Stalin and the Stavka agree to defend first and counterattack afterwards</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The German offensive opens on the northern and southern faces of the salient</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the German advance in the north is halted</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The Soviets launch Operation Kutuzov, and the armoured clash at Prokhorovka is fought on the southern face</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1943</span><span class="rf-text">Operation Roland ends and the II SS Panzer Corps is ordered to withdraw</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1943</span><span class="rf-text">Operation Polkovodets Rumyantsev opens against the southern wing of Army Group South</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1943</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> is liberated after heavy fighting</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-kursk" data-v="f10860b335" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies take up their ground</b> The Germans planned two pincers on the Kursk salient, the 9th Army from the north and the 4th Panzer Army with Army Detachment Kempf from the south, while the 2nd Army held the west. The Central and Voronezh Fronts stood in fortified belts, with the Steppe Front in reserve.</li><li><b>Both German pincers attack</b> The 9th Army attacked the Central Front in the north, and in the south the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf attacked across the Voronezh Front's sector. Each broke into the first belt of the Soviet defences.</li><li><b>The northern attack is held at Ponyri and Olkhovatka</b> Model put the weight of the 9th Army on Ponyri and Olkhovatka, and the Central Front counterattacked and held both. By 10 July the Soviets had completely halted the German advance in the north.</li><li><b>Soviet reserves arrive and the II SS turns to Prokhorovka</b> The 5th Guards Tank Army left the Steppe Front for Prokhorovka on 7 July. On 10 July Hoth turned the II SS Panzer Corps of the 4th Panzer Army north-east towards it, and on 11 July Army Detachment Kempf broke through.</li><li><b>The tank battle at Prokhorovka</b> The 5th Guards Tank Army attacked down the slopes before Prokhorovka against the II SS Panzer Corps, which was attacking on its own plan. Neither side accomplished its objectives that day.</li><li><b>Kutuzov opens in the north and Citadel ends in the south</b> The Bryansk and Western Fronts began Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient, threatening the 9th Army's flank and rear, so that it went over fully to the defensive. In the south the II SS Panzer Corps was ordered on 17 July to end its offensive and begin to withdraw.</li><li><b>Operation Rumyantsev drives south to Belgorod</b> The Voronezh and Steppe Fronts attacked the northern wing of Army Group South, aiming to destroy the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf. They drove through the German positions and by 5 August had liberated Belgorod.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad.jpg" alt="Battle of Stalingrad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Расчет_советской_76,2-мм_пушки_ЗиС-3_ведет_огонь_в_Сталинграде._Ноябрь_1942_г.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> went over to a general offensive in the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> was retaken by the Soviets on 8 February 1943, but Field Marshal Erich von Manstein, given freedom of action, counterattacked from 21 February and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> on 15 March and Belgorod on 18 March. The spring rasputitsa and the exhaustion of both armies ended operations by mid-March, leaving a Soviet salient about 250 kilometres from north to south and 160 kilometres from east to west, centred on Kursk.</p><p>On 15 April Hitler issued Operational Order No. 6, calling for the offensive codenamed Zitadelle (Citadel). It was meant to weaken Soviet offensive strength for the summer, and Hitler believed a victory would restore German prestige with allies he thought were considering withdrawing from the war. It was also hoped that many Soviet prisoners would be taken for use as slave labour in German armaments factories. Model, Guderian and later Manstein doubted it, and Hitler postponed it several times to wait for new tanks, until 5 July.</p><p>The Soviet side knew of the plan. The Lucy spy ring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> passed on intelligence, which John Cairncross confirmed by forwarding raw decrypts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a>. Stalin and some officers wished to strike first, but Zhukov and others advised a strategic defensive. In April Stalin and the Stavka agreed to wear out the German attack before counterattacking.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kursk&#8221; cites where it covers Background, German plans and preparation, Soviet plans and preparation, Contest for aerial supremacy:</p><ul><li>David M. Glantz, The Battle of Kursk (University Press of Kansas, 2004)</li><li>Bruno Kasdorf, The Battle of Kursk – An Analysis of Strategic and Operational Principles (U.S. Army War College, 2000)</li><li>Robert M. Citino, The Wehrmacht Retreats: Fighting a Lost War, 1943 (University Press of Kansas, 2012)</li><li>David M. Glantz, When Titans Clashed: How the Red Army Stopped Hitler (University of Kansas Press, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German force amounted to around 777,000 men, 2,451 tanks and assault guns, which was 70 percent of the German armour on the Eastern Front, and 7,417 guns and mortars. By another count the total was 777,000 to 779,000 men. In the north Model's 9th Army had 335,000 men and 988 tanks and assault guns. In the south Hoth's 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf, under Werner Kempf, had 1,377. About 259 Panthers, 211 Tigers and 90 Ferdinands were used. The force was markedly short of infantry, and the Panthers came straight from training, with little time to learn the terrain.</p><p>The Soviets prepared three main defensive belts in each of the Central Front's and Voronezh Front's sectors, backed by three fallback belts, with the whole system nearly 300 kilometres deep, including lines manned by the Steppe Front. More than 300,000 civilians worked on it. The engineers laid 503,993 anti-tank and 439,348 anti-personnel mines. Nearly all artillery was given an anti-tank role, and infantry were trained against 'tank phobia' by having tanks driven over them in trenches.</p><p>The Central Front under Rokossovsky held the northern face, the Voronezh Front under Vatutin the southern, and Konev's Steppe Front was in reserve. Soviet strength was 1,910,361 men, of whom 1,426,352 were combat soldiers, and 4,869 tanks and 259 self-propelled guns. About a third of the tanks were light. The T-34's 76.2 mm gun struggled against the frontal armour of Tigers and Panthers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kursk&#8221; cites where it covers German plans and preparation, Soviet plans and preparation, Germans, Red Army:</p><ul><li>David M. Glantz, The Battle of Kursk (University Press of Kansas, 2004)</li><li>Robert M. Citino, The Wehrmacht Retreats: Fighting a Lost War, 1943 (University Press of Kansas, 2012)</li><li>Bruno Kasdorf, The Battle of Kursk – An Analysis of Strategic and Operational Principles (U.S. Army War College, 2000)</li><li>Mark Healy, Kursk 1943: Tide Turns in the East (Osprey, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fighting began on the evening of 4 July on the southern face, and Zhukov ordered a pre-emptive bombardment at around 02:00 on 5 July, which delayed the Germans but did not disrupt their schedule. The Germans attacked on both faces that morning. In the north Model used infantry divisions reinforced with assault guns and heavy tanks, keeping his panzer divisions back for exploitation. Minefields slowed them, and by the end of the first day they had advanced 5 to 6 miles at a cost of 1,287 killed and missing and 5,921 wounded. Fighting then centred on Ponyri and Olkhovatka for three days. By 10 July the Soviets had halted the advance.</p><p>In the south the main attack was delivered by Hoth's 4th Panzer Army. The II SS Panzer Corps broke through the first Soviet belt by 09:00 on 5 July, but the 3rd SS Division made slow progress and Army Detachment Kempf was delayed at the Donets. Soviet counterattacks on 8 July were piecemeal and failed, but stalled the II SS Panzer Corps. On 9 July German units reached the Psel River, and the corps turned north-east towards Prokhorovka.</p><p>On 12 July, at about 08:30, Rotmistrov ordered the 5th Guards Tank Army to attack at Prokhorovka. It failed with heavy losses, though it stopped a German breakthrough, and by the end of the day the Soviets had fallen back to their starting positions. That evening Hitler summoned Kluge and Manstein and stopped the offensive.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kursk&#8221; cites where it covers Preliminary actions, Red Army counter-attack, Development of the battle, Battle of Prokhorovka:</p><ul><li>David M. Glantz, The Battle of Kursk (University Press of Kansas, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hitler ended the offensive because the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> two days earlier and he feared further landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> or southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Manstein was reluctant, believing his forces were on the verge of breaking through. Hitler agreed to let the southern attack go on, then moved Manstein's reserve south. Operation Roland began on 14 July, and on 17 July the II SS Panzer Corps was ordered to withdraw, one division going to Italy.</p><p>On 12 July the Soviets launched Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient, which threatened Model's 9th Army and forced it onto the defensive. On 3 August Operation Polkovodets Rumyantsev began against the southern wing of Army Group South. The Soviets liberated Belgorod on 5 August and reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> by 12 August, where a counter-attack by the 2nd and 3rd SS Panzer Divisions halted them. Kharkov was liberated on 23 August.</p><p>Krivosheyev's figures, which Glantz considers the most reliable, give Soviet losses of 177,877 in the German offensive. The two Soviet offensives cost 685,456. Frieser calculates 54,182 German casualties during Citadel, and about 170,000 for the three battles. Soviet tank losses were 1,614 of 3,925 during the German offensive. Hitler blamed the defeat on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kursk&#8221; cites where it covers Results, Casualties, Soviet losses, German losses:</p><ul><li>Karl-Heinz Frieser, Germany and the Second World War: The Eastern Front 1943–1944: The War in the East and on the Neighbouring Fronts (Oxford University Press, 2017)</li><li>David M. Glantz, The Battle of Kursk (University Press of Kansas, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Casualty figures are uncertain for both sides. German equipment losses are hard to count because the Germans tried to recover and repair tanks, so a tank disabled one day might be back in action the next. German personnel records were seized at the end of the war; many went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> national archives and were not available until 1978, while others were taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, which declined to confirm that they existed.</p><p>Historians disagree on the Soviet dead. Töppel says official figures are underestimated by 40%, which gives 1.2 million, and cites Russian critics with estimates from 910,000 to 2.3 million. Sokolov gives 450,000 killed, 50,000 missing and 1.2 million wounded, which Glantz says may be inflated, though he accepts that the official figures are probably conservative. Many Russian historians consider Sokolov unreliable. Estimates of German losses over the whole battle range from 380,000 to 430,000 and, per Bellamy and Hastings, more than half a million.</p><p>Schönbach argues that Western historians have used German loss statistics without critical analysis, because 'total losses' were recorded as tanks under repair, and that 40% to 50% of German tanks were caught in a repair backlog. Estimates of tanks destroyed vary: Frieser has 760 and Töppel up to 1,200. For aircraft, Frieser calls the official Soviet figure of 1,626 'completely unbelievable' and Bergström counts 1,104 Soviet aircraft lost.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was the first time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> that a German strategic offensive was halted before it could break through enemy defences and penetrate to its strategic depths. It was the last strategic offensive the Germans were able to launch on the Eastern Front. Guderian wrote that the failure of Zitadelle was a decisive defeat, after which the enemy was in undisputed possession of the initiative. The counter-offensives were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s first successful summer offensives of the war.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> has been called the single largest battle in the history of warfare and the single deadliest armoured battle in history. The opening day, 5 July, was the costliest day in aerial warfare in terms of aircraft shot down. It is cited by some as the true turning point of the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, though this view has frequently been criticised. Robin Cross wrote that Kursk dealt the Ostheer heavier psychological and material blows than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>.</p><p>Hitler's interference in German military matters grew, while Stalin, trusting commanders whose decisions brought success, stepped back from operational planning, so that the Red Army was given more autonomy. A total of 239 Red Army personnel were made Hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> for the battle, among them two women medics, Mariya Borovichenko and Zinaida Mareseva, posthumously.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> in July 1943 the Germans attacked a Soviet salient and were halted by deep defences prepared with advance knowledge of the plan. Soviet counter-offensives at Orel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> kept the initiative thereafter.</em></p>

